ID: B3-Ch10c-Collector
TEXT:

 "You won't ever forget it?" Eiden's smile widens by a fraction. "Not even if I offer you a rare find from my travels?"

He draws a smooth, dark mineral from his pocket and extends it on an open palm. Melindra lifts her chin, fighting for indifference, but her gaze locks onto the stone and stays there.

"Even for a collector of your taste, pieces like this do not surface often," Eiden says, turning it across his knuckles. "What was your name for them again? Deitrah... Dengah..."

"A Dedra stone," Flower and I blurt together.

"Ah, a Dedra stone!" Eiden's eyebrows lift, genuinely amused.

His gaze drifts from Flower to me, then slides past my shoulder toward Kate. She stands motionless, frost crusting her knuckles, tracking his distance from Melindra.

"Congratulations, Melindra," Eiden says. "It seems you found decent company while I was away."

"I'll take the stone," Melindra snaps, snatching it from his palm before he can withdraw it. A faint flush touches her ears. "Consider it an apology. But you are not forgiven."

"Of course," Eiden says, smooth and polite.

 "Are you going to explain why you are here," Illuna says, her voice cold and level from Flower's mouth, "or should we start guessing?"

"All in good time, Illuna of the sacred woods massacre," Eiden replies. "First, an unrelated curiosity. Was there not an armored white mage traveling with you? Whatever became of Daren? Tall, dark-skinned, encased in steel from sunrise to sundown?"

"I am standing right in front of you," Daren says. "Stop pretending you cannot see me."

"My good sir, you must be mistaken," Eiden says. "Beyond the conspicuous absence of plate armor, the Daren I know would have drawn steel by now, or at the very least glared at me as though measuring my throat. I recall no pleasant conversations between us since our last meeting, so what has changed?"

"Right. You wouldn't remember," Daren murmurs, crossing his arms. "You have no way of remembering."

"Pardon?"

"Let us say a different version of you, in another world, offered me a hand when I had hit rock bottom," Daren says. "Because of that, I am inclined toward patience. For now."

Eiden's polite expression tightens. "...You speak of the God of Time's trials."

"Yes," Daren says.

 "I see." Eiden falls silent, the humor draining from his features. "In that case, take this as fair warning. That Eiden and I share neither memory nor mind. Depending on how far back the ritual reached, that version of me may have held a view of this world entirely alien to mine. Whatever he promised or confessed inside that trial does not apply here. Build your hopes on it and you will only set yourself up for disappointment."

"Do not worry," Daren says. "I have no intention of confusing the two."

"Good," Eiden says, the easy smile sliding back into place. "With that settled, let us turn to the first matter that brought me here. Ollendor. Tell me, Barry, what do you think about their new king?"

choice(""I think he will be a great leader."", B3-Ch10c-Leader, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Leader, v_b3_ch10_leader = 1)
choice(""I don't really care that much about him, to be honest."", B3-Ch10c-Honest, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Honest, v_b3_ch10_leader = 0)
choice(""I think he was the least bad choice available at the time."", B3-Ch10c-Available, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Available, v_b3_ch10_leader = 2)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Leader
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch10_behind,1) if (v_b3_ch4_cut == 0 && v_b3_ch8_king == 5 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0)
achievement("No man left behind",v_ac_b3_ch10_behind)
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 0 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
 "I think he will be a great leader."

"The underground king's right hand?" Eiden arches a brow. "That is your verdict?"

"He beats the predecessor," I say, shrugging.

"A low bar," Eiden remarks. "I am asking if you believe he was the best choice on the table."

"Why not?" I say. "Albert ran the rebel network before he ever served the underground king. He knows how to hold an organization together."

"I see," Eiden says.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 1 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
 "I think he will be a great leader."

"The underground king's right hand?" Eiden arches a brow. "That is your verdict?"

"He beats the predecessor," I say, shrugging.

"A low bar," Eiden remarks. "I am asking if you believe he was the best choice on the table."

"Why not?" I say. "Peter was the most popular figure among the rebels, and easily the most decent of the three. He has the temperament to rebuild the kingdom."

"I see," Eiden says.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 2 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0 || v_b3_ch8_king == 3 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0 || v_b3_ch8_king == 4 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
 "I think he will be a great leader."

"The king of the underground?" Eiden arches a brow. "That is your verdict?"

"He beats the predecessor," I say, shrugging.

"A low bar," Eiden remarks. "I am asking if you believe he was the best choice on the table."

"Why not?" I say. "The man has spent decades running a city beneath a city. He knows how power functions."

"I see," Eiden says.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 7 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 8) {
 "I think he will be a great leader."

"The Overseer?" Eiden taps his chin. "Last I checked, he was drafting more mage captives into the seredium towers to boost drone output."

"That bastard," I mutter. "He swore the tower issue would be settled."

"And it was," Eiden says. "From the Overseer's perspective, the sole issue was operating below capacity. He resolved it, precisely as promised."

"He will regret it," I say. "The moment we have the strength to tear him apart, we return to Ollendor and leave him in the same ditch as the last king."

"I see," Eiden says.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
 "I think he will be a great leader."

"Breynan or the Overseer?" Eiden asks.

"Both of them," I say. "They balance each other out. As long as they cooperate, the city stands a chance."

"Fascinating," Eiden murmurs. "Word reaches me that the Overseer is re-engineering the towers to run without shackled mages. A peculiar waste of resources for a machine with no moral compass. Did your group negotiate that?"

"It was the condition for our help," I say. "He agreed to unhook the prisoners the moment he devises an alternate power feed."

"Interesting," Eiden says.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 5 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
 "I think he will be a great leader."

"A goblin healer on the throne is an inventive choice," Eiden says. "I was equally intrigued by the underground king's sudden cooperative streak during the transition."

"What do you mean?" I ask. "What happened?"

"You have not heard?" Eiden asks. "Your goblin companion insisted on an immediate release of every captive in the seredium towers. Had he severed them instantly, the entire infrastructure would have collapsed—flying transports plummeting, wards failing, total panic. To prevent riots, the underground king seeded rumors across the districts for two days, exposing the torture cells and the previous monarch's atrocities. By the time the towers powered down, crowds were marching to demand the mages' release. Your goblin simply granted the public will."

"Clever," I say. "If the citizens demanded the shutdown themselves, they could hardly revolt when the flying transports stopped running."

"There were still grievances, but far fewer than a sudden blackout would have sparked," Eiden says. "The odds of civil war dropped to near zero. A remarkable outcome, given the volatility."

"Good," I say.

Silence settles between us for several seconds until I break it.

"Is that all you wanted to ask?"

"On that subject, yes," Eiden says.

"And the second matter?" Kate asks, her brow furrowing.

"A timely question, Kate of the Beacon of Hope," Eiden says. "This concerns you above all others. Waiting for your little band to reach your next milestone grew tedious, so I came to quicken your pace. Your upcoming encounter interests me as much as it does you."

"Why would our next destination interest you?" Kate asks.

"...You do not know where you are going?" Eiden's eyes remain closed, but his head tilts in genuine surprise.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king != 5 || v_b3_ch8_overseer2 != 0) {
 Silence settles between us for several seconds until I break it.

"Is that all you wanted to ask?"

"On that subject, yes," Eiden says.

"And the second matter?" Kate asks, her brow furrowing.

"A timely question, Kate of the Beacon of Hope," Eiden says. "This concerns you above all others. Waiting for your little band to reach your next milestone grew tedious, so I came to quicken your pace. Your upcoming encounter interests me as much as it does you."

"Why would our next destination interest you?" Kate asks.

"...You do not know where you are going?" Eiden's eyes remain closed, but his head tilts in genuine surprise.

He turns toward Melindra. Her posture stiffens instantly.

}
 "Melindra, you have not told them?" Eiden asks.

"I was going to!" Melindra says, her voice jumping an octave. "I was waiting for the right moment!"

"There is no time like the present," Eiden says smoothly.

"Melindra, what is this?" Kate steps forward. "Who are you taking us to? What does this have to do with me?"

"Kate, listen to me," Melindra says, hands fluttering in front of her chest. "First, stay calm. Promise me you will stay calm."

"Fine," Kate says. "I promise."

"Right," Melindra takes a ragged breath. "When we struck our deal in Ollendor, I knew nothing about artificial mages or the Beacon of Hope. I only learned about all that later, listening to you talk around the fire."

"Understood," Kate says. "Why bring it up now?"

"Because..." Melindra swallows hard. "The contact with the pinecone detector... my acquaintance... she is--"

"Meridith," Eiden cuts in, sparing her the agony. "She is taking you to Meridith."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Message, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Message)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Honest
TEXT:

 "I don't really care that much about him, to be honest."

"You don't?" Eiden's brow arches. "Curious. I was under the impression your company held considerable sway over Ollendor's succession."

"We pushed a few pieces around, but it hardly matters," I say. "The old tyrant is dead. Whoever sits on the chair is almost guaranteed to be an improvement."

"I see," Eiden says.

Silence settles between us for several seconds until I break it.

"Is that all you wanted to ask?"

"On that subject, yes," Eiden says.

"And the second matter?" Kate asks, her brow furrowing.

"A timely question, Kate of the Beacon of Hope," Eiden says. "This concerns you above all others. Waiting for your little band to reach your next milestone grew tedious, so I came to quicken your pace. Your upcoming encounter interests me as much as it does you."

"Why would our next destination interest you?" Kate asks.

"...You do not know where you are going?" Eiden's eyes remain closed, but his head tilts in genuine surprise.

He turns toward Melindra. Her posture stiffens instantly.

 "Melindra, you have not told them?" Eiden asks.

"I was going to!" Melindra says, her voice jumping an octave. "I was waiting for the right moment!"

"There is no time like the present," Eiden says smoothly.

