choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Message, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Message)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Message
TEXT:

 "Meridith as in... the leader of the lessathi?" Kate's voice drops low.

"I am so sorry!" Melindra's words spill out in a rush. "I swear I knew nothing about your past with her when we made our deal in Ollendor! When I figured it out, I tried to handle it alone and fetch the pinecone detector without dragging you into it, but she wouldn't budge. She grilled me on the transceiver about why I needed the locator and who I was traveling with. The moment I mentioned Daren, she connected the dots, figured out you were with us, and demanded to see you. She said unless you ask for the device in person, I get nothing. Kate, I know how terrible this sounds, but give me time! I can fix this!"

Behind her, Daren, Hadrik, Flower, and Illuna exchange sharp, tense whispers. From the golden amulet around Flower's neck, Arraka's laughter rings out, shrill and delighted. I catch Eiden's smirk turned squarely in my direction, his eyelids shut as ever.

"Aren't you pleased, Barry?" Eiden murmurs. "Now you finally have the opportunity to deliver my message."

Eiden's message. With the chaos in Ollendor, it had slipped my mind entirely. He still expects me to hand it over.

 "Melindra, look at me," Kate says, gripping Melindra's shoulders. "Take a deep breath and let your shoulders drop. Do it now."

"Right. Deep breath. Doing it," Melindra pants, chest heaving in exaggerated gulps of air that do nothing to steady her pulse. "What now?"

"Listen carefully," Kate says. "Meeting Meridith face to face is fine. Set up the rendezvous."

"It's... fine?" Melindra blinks, disoriented. "You mean it?"

"A week ago I might have refused," Kate says. "During the God of Time's first trial, Meridith kept me alive. Without her intervention, I would not have survived the chamber. I owe her the benefit of the doubt, especially when Diane told me she chose to work alongside her freely."

"So I can contact her?" Melindra asks. "Tell her we will meet whenever she is ready?"

"Yes," Kate says.

"The relief..." Melindra lets out a long breath. "I thought you were going to draw a blade on me. Pin me to a trunk with a knife at my throat."

 "I would never do that to a friend," Kate says evenly.

Her eyes flick to me, a pointed look that makes it very clear she knows exactly who told Melindra about the dagger incident in the forest.

"Isn't that right, Barry?"

"Naturally," I say quickly. "Melindra was imagining worst-case scenarios. Nothing to worry about."

"Then I am going to set up the channel right now," Melindra says.

"Go," Kate tells her.

"Back in a moment," Melindra calls out as an updraft catches her clothes, lifting her into the canopy.

"That concludes my business with your little circle," Eiden says, stepping back. "We will cross paths again soon enough."

"You know where we are," Daren says.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Revenant, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Revenant) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Condition, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Condition) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Revenant
TEXT:

 "One last curiosity before I take my leave," Eiden says, pausing mid-step. "Do any of you know why your invisible revenant friend has been hovering behind me with a dagger drawn and her aura clamped shut for the last ten minutes?"

"Damn it, I hoped he would miss her," Arraka grumbles from the amulet as light bends and the revenant materializes in the clearing.

"Rose..." Kate's voice tightens at the sight of the familiar face worn by a corpse.

"Can I assist you with something?" Eiden asks her, head tilted.

"My intent was to freeze time and strike at your most vulnerable moment, as you initiated your teleportation," the revenant states in a flat, hollow cadence. "That window is closed."

"A servant of the God of Death, if I am not mistaken," Eiden says. "Why would your deity seek my demise?"

 "My master learned through a reliable source that you orchestrated the disruption on the magical plane earlier today," the revenant replies. "He demands retribution for your arrogance."

The disruption earlier today. Jirinn must have already set the gods at each other's throats, exactly as Arraka outlined.

"I have no notion of what you speak of," Eiden says, faintly puzzled. "I have not meddled in the affairs of the magical plane for centuries."

"My mandate is execution, not interrogation," she says.

"By all means," Eiden smirks. "Give it your best effort."

She vanishes. In the same heartbeat she snaps into place beside Eiden, steel flashing toward his throat—and ricochets backward through the air as an invisible barrier erupts, slamming her into the turf without Eiden lifting a finger.

"Did you assume I wander about without automated countermeasures against temporal manipulation?" Eiden asks. "The God of Death lacks ambition if he dispatches a lone knife to take my head."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Revenant2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Revenant2)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Revenant2
TEXT:

 "The God of Death... does not... trust me..." The revenant pushes herself off the dirt, every syllable strained after the impact. "I am merely... a disposable tool. A test... of allegiance."

"How pathetic," Eiden remarks.

"I am not... finished." She flickers and vanishes, reappearing behind Flower in the same breath, blade pressed flush against the girl's neck.

"Rose?" Flower tilts her head back, looking up into dead eyes.

"I apologize," the revenant says tonelessly. "The God of Death commanded me to secure you as leverage if the assassination failed."

"It is fine," Flower says softly. "I do not blame you."

 "Release her," Daren commands, his blade clearing its scabbard in a single, fluid arc.

"The captive remains with me until the stillwater complies," she states. "Not a second before."

"And why should her survival concern me?" Eiden asks.

"My master knows the golden fox requested your protection on her behalf," the revenant says. "Yield, or she dies."

"Your intelligence is flawed," Eiden replies. "Eleya asked that I refrain from killing her myself. She said nothing of playing guardian."

"She dies if you refuse!" the revenant presses.

 "Go right ahead," Eiden says, his smile widening. "You would do me a service. She remains the sole vessel capable of housing Arraka if that amulet ever shatters."

A heavy silence drops over the clearing. The revenant stares at him, searching for a tremor in his stance.

"Proceed," Eiden urges quietly. "What stays your hand?"

The blade bites slightly deeper into Flower's skin, but the revenant's fingers twitch with hesitation. Despite claiming to be nothing more than Death's instrument, some remnant of choice lingers in her. An opening to talk her down.

choice("Try to reason with the revenant.", B3-Ch10c-Revenant3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Revenant3, v_b3_ch10_reason = 1)
choice("Threaten the revenant.", B3-Ch10c-Revenant3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Revenant3, v_b3_ch10_reason = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Revenant3
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch10_reasonable,1) if (v_b3_ch10_reason == 1)
achievement("Let's be reasonable!",v_ac_b3_ch10_reasonable)
#if(v_b3_ch10_reason == 1) {
 "Think about what you are doing," I call out, keeping my voice steady. "Is this the God of Death's actual purpose? To slaughter Flower for nothing when Eiden refuses to care? Throwing away your only leverage solves nothing. There has to be another path."

"Another path..." she repeats, her grip slackening. The edge eases away from Flower's throat. "Yes... another path."

Flower does not hesitate. She ducks beneath the revenant's elbow, breaks clear, and kicks off the ground with twin blasts of fire roaring from the soles of her feet, shooting straight into the air well out of reach.

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_reason == 0) {
 "Take that blade off her neck this second," I say, stepping forward, "or you will not survive the day."

"Your threats carry no weight," she says, her resolve hardening. "The will of Death is absolute. If this vessel falls, it is a necessary offering for--"

A whip of solid water snaps out from behind her, wrapping tight around her knife wrist and yanking it backward. Flower ducks under the pinned arm and vaults clear, ignition roaring from the soles of her feet as fire jets launch her high above the clearing.

}
 The moment Flower clears the ground, Eiden's aura constricts around the revenant's throat. Invisible pressure lifts her several feet into the air.

"That reminds me," Eiden says, watching her claw at empty air as her temporal tricks fail to gain purchase. "There was an experiment regarding time weavers I intended to conduct years ago. I began setting it up with Rose while she lived, but something else drew my attention."

"Stop!" Kate yells, stepping between them. "Leave her alone!"

"Though crushing your windpipe is pointless, is it not?" Eiden muses, observing the corpse writhing midair. "Undead flesh requires no oxygen. A change of tactic, then."

A crescent of compressed wind flashes from his fingers, severing her head in a clean, silent arc.

The sequence dissolves into an incohesive blur. Voices echo—Eiden, Daren, Kate arguing over something distant—like memories viewed through murky water.

Just as when Daren struck her down in the God of Time's ritual, the aftermath lingers as a half-remembered dream. Yet the revenant stands whole once more, planted on the exact patch of grass she occupied before the choke began. The Magium's contingency reversed time strictly for her and her killer, leaving the rest of us with nothing but hazy impressions of an erased timeline.

"Fascinating," Eiden remarks, his smile returning. "Theory holds that the Magium preserves its prophets until their verse is spoken, but witnessing the mechanism is another matter entirely. The weave rewound just far enough to afford you an exit. What happens if I cut you down repeatedly, I wonder? Does the ward fatigue, or does it hold indefinitely? Shall we test its limits?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Revenant4, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Revenant4)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Revenant4
TEXT:

 Before Eiden gathers his aura for another strike, the revenant vanishes. Her signature evaporates entirely from the surrounding woods.

"She withdrew," Eiden says, sighing with mild disappointment. "Pity."

He pockets both hands, his polite smile settling back in place.

"Farewell," he says, and space folds around him as he disappears.

"Arraka, confirm it," I say. "Is he gone, or just hovering behind a concealment ward?"

"He left," Arraka replies from the amulet. "He knows better than to play invisibility tricks while I am watching."

 "Her timing was absurdly precise," Daren says, scanning the treeline. "She needed leverage against Eiden, and she materialized the exact hour he dropped in on us."

"The God of Death may hold island-wide awareness like Eiden," Illuna says. "Or our party is being monitored directly. The golden fox maintained a link on us until Eiden stripped it away."

"No one is tracking you," Arraka snaps. "The only reason I stayed quiet about the fox was boredom. That calculation has changed. I have zero interest in giving the gods a front-row seat to our strategy sessions."

 "Even so," Illuna says, "what troubles me is that Death knew of Eiden's promise to the fox regarding Flower—a detail we never possessed. Where does one uncover that caliber of intelligence?"

"Gods keep eyes on Eiden," Arraka says. "He has crossed paths with their cultists for six centuries. They track him as best they can. A creature of his strength cannot sneeze without half the magical plane taking notes."

"Let us hope that is the extent of it," Illuna mutters.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Condition, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Condition)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Condition
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "Indeed," Eiden says. "I always do. Farewell."

He smiles, slips his hands into his pockets, and vanishes in a fold of displaced air.

"Arraka, confirm it," I say. "Is he gone, or just hovering behind a concealment ward?"

"He left," Arraka replies from the amulet. "He knows better than to play invisibility tricks while I am watching."

"We should prepare for the lessathi leader," Kate says. "We have no idea what escort she travels with. Better to assume hostility."

While Kate, Flower, and I strike the tents, Hadrik helps Daren stow his plate armor into his pack, as planned. We finish packing just as Melindra drops out of the sky, her brow furrowed.

"I reached Meridith," she says, touching down. "She added an extra condition before she agrees to sit down with us. She said-- wait. Where is Eiden?"

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "We should prepare for the lessathi leader," Kate says. "We have no idea what escort she travels with. Better to assume hostility."

While Kate, Flower, and I strike the tents, Hadrik helps Daren stow his plate armor into his pack, as planned. We finish packing just as Melindra drops out of the sky, her brow furrowed.

"I reached Meridith," she says, touching down. "She added an extra condition before she agrees to sit down with us. She said-- wait. Where is Eiden?"

}
 "He departed while you were aloft," Kate tells her.

"He left?" Melindra's voice spikes. "He couldn't spare three minutes to speak with me? Not even a word of parting? I am going to make him pay for that."

"You mentioned a condition," Hadrik prompts.

"Right. Meridith demands we meet her in their primary subterranean stronghold," Melindra says. "They maintain a heavy teleportation array to pull us down, but they must send a guide to lead us to a designated beacon coordinate where the array can lock onto our transceivers."

"She wants us inside their main installation?" Daren's jaw sets. "That sounds like a trap waiting to snap shut."

"I agree," Kate says. "Risking the entire group is foolish. I should go alone. Meridith asked for me specifically."

choice("Tell Kate that it's too dangerous to go there alone.", B3-Ch10c-Brother, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Brother, v_b3_ch10_worry = 1)
choice("Say nothing, and wait to see how the team reacts to Kate's proposal.", B3-Ch10c-Brother, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Brother, v_b3_ch10_worry = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Brother
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_worry == 1 && v_b3_ch1_kate3 == 0) {
 "You are not walking into that lair alone," I say. "It is far too dangerous."

"I can take care of myself," Kate replies. "There is no reason to fret over me."

"I will worry about you as much as I please, thanks," I say.

Color climbs swiftly into her cheeks. She turns her face aside, staring into the brush to hide it.

My mind flashes back to our conversation after the God of Time's ritual, when she tried to bury the mental strain and I told her not to lock me out because worrying about her was my choice.

"Barry is correct," Daren cuts in. "Sending you in alone is out of the question. We can attempt to negotiate terms with the courier Meridith dispatches, but if they refuse compromise, we swallow the risk together. We have come too far to abandon the locator now."

A tendril of solidified water lashes around Kate's waist, anchoring her in place. She spins around, startled.

"Illuna, what are you doing?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_worry == 1 && v_b3_ch1_kate3 != 0) {
 "You are not walking into that lair alone," I say. "It is far too dangerous."

"I can take care of myself," Kate replies. "There is no reason to fret over me."

"I will worry about you as much as I please, thanks," I say.

"Barry is correct," Daren cuts in. "Sending you in alone is out of the question. We can attempt to negotiate terms with the courier Meridith dispatches, but if they refuse compromise, we swallow the risk together. We have come too far to abandon the locator now."

A tendril of solidified water lashes around Kate's waist, anchoring her in place. She spins around, startled.

"Illuna, what are you doing?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_worry == 0) {
 "That is not an option," Daren tells Kate firmly. "Either the entire company enters the stronghold, or none of us do."

"You doubt my capabilities?" Kate frowns.

"Not your skill, but the odds," Daren replies. "Marching isolated into a subterranean fortress of potential hostiles invites disaster. Even as a united front, negotiation will be fraught."

A tendril of solidified water lashes around Kate's waist, anchoring her in place. She spins around, startled.

"Illuna, what are you doing?"

}
 "We established back in Ollendor that martyrdom is off the table," Illuna says, hauling on the water lash to reel Kate in. "Did that pact slip your memory? Must I keep you leashed to prevent you from wandering into traps the moment our backs are turned?"

"Fine, I yield!" Kate calls out, hauled a step closer. "I retract the proposal. Release the whip."

"Hmph." The water whip dissolves into vapor. "Do not test my patience again."

"Melindra, any guess as to who Meridith will dispatch?" Daren asks.

"None," Melindra says. "Every past rendezvous brought a different escort. The extraction coordinate lies ahead. Follow me."

"Is now an acceptable time to ask how you connected with the lessathi leadership in the first place?" Hadrik asks as we fall in behind her.

"My brother maintained dealings with her," Melindra says. "He facilitated our introduction."

"If you have walked this land for six centuries and your brother still lives, does that make him a stillwater as well?" Kate asks.

 "Yes," Melindra says. "He and I were the only half-lessathi present when--"

She cuts herself off, catching the slip.

"We were the only two half-lessathi among those who became stillwaters," she clarifies smoothly. "The rest were human casters, Eiden included."

"And I imagine you remain as tight-lipped regarding how stillwaters are made as during our previous inquiries," Daren notes.

"Naturally," Melindra says. "And it serves your survival. Eiden systematically erased every scrap of documentation surrounding that process to ensure another still winter never recurs. If he suspected you held even a fragment of that truth, your lives would be forfeit."

"A fair point," Daren concedes. "Our list of mortal adversaries is long enough without adding another. Do we turn here?"

"Around this thicket, then west," Melindra directs. "Nearly there."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Designated, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Designated)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Designated
TEXT:

 When we break into the clearing, the envoy is already waiting. But she wears no lessathi robes. Instead, standing before us is a young woman of twenty-three with a wiry, muscular build, a burn scar tracing down her left forearm, and cropped blond hair crackling with static electricity that lifts the strands on end.

"Diane!" Kate calls out. "You're really here!"

"You never listen," Diane says, sighing with weary affection. "I explicitly told you to leave the island, yet here you are, marching straight back into--"

Kate cuts her off with a fierce, crushing embrace, wrapping both arms around her before Diane can step back.

"I have you," Kate says, burying her face into Diane's shoulder. "You aren't slipping away again. Not this time."

"Hey—stop, let go!" Diane laughs, struggling against Kate's iron grip. "You are embarrassing me in front of your company!"

 "Good to see you again," Hadrik rumbles, stepping forward with a grin. "Though you caused us a mountain of grief, girl. Knocking us cold in the sacred woods left us straight in the golden fox's jaws. Nearly died three times over."

"You did?" Diane wriggles free of Kate's hold, her eyes going wide with alarm. "I had no idea—I didn't consider--"

"Bwahaha!" Hadrik boomed. "Relax! Just poking at you. We came through in one piece, with grand tales to show for it. All's well that ends well!"

"Right..." Diane rubs the back of her neck, unsettled. "Still, I owe everyone an apology. My conduct was unacceptable. I was frantic to keep Kate away from the lessathi and reacted without thinking. Under pressure, I default to terrible decisions. An old failing I haven't managed to outgrow. Truly, I am sorry. I will make it right."

 "If keeping Kate away from lessathi hands was your priority," Daren says, "why do you serve them? Are you under coercion?"

"No," Diane says. "To be precise, I work alongside Meridith. Not the rest of her people."

"Is she not their ruler?" Hadrik asks.

"In title," Diane says. "Their society fractured decades ago, following Lord Heksol's death. The traditionalists avoid outright rebellion, but they actively undermine Meridith's policies at every turn. Meridith believes she can gradually reform the culture from within. I find her optimism misplaced."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Advanced, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Advanced)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Advanced
TEXT:

 "Why remain here at all?" Kate asks softly. "Why not cross the ocean and build a life? Is Jason the reason?"

"Partly," Diane admits. "My brother idolizes Meridith. Nothing would convince him to abandon her. But beyond that, I saw firsthand what lessathi extremism creates. I will not allow another Beacon of Hope to emerge. Meridith dismantled the facility, outlawed the execution of runaways, and banned the disposal of disabled infants. She forced decency onto an empire that had none. I respect her for that fight, and that is why I stand with her."

"A complicated picture," Daren says. "Meridith sounds reasonable, but she governs a hostile populace barely held in check. What prevents them from ambushing us the moment we materialize in their territory?"

 "Daren raises a valid concern," Kate says. "Diane, is entering their central base truly safe?"

"You need not worry," Diane replies evenly.

"Why?" Kate asks.

"Because if any of them lay a finger on you, I will slaughter them," Diane says. Her pupils flare with a blazing gold light. "I will tear through their ranks, and they are fully aware of what I can do. They will not dare touch you."

Flower gasps, pointing directly at Diane's face.

"You have the glowing eye trick!" Flower's face lights up. "I thought I was the only one! Watch, I can do it too!"

Flower clenches both fists, focusing her aura until heat ripples off her shoulders. Her eyes ignite into a fierce, radiant crimson.

"That... is an advanced ethereal discipline," Diane says, her gaze lingering on the intensity of Flower's flames. "Mastering that resonance usually demands decades of dedicated training. To achieve it at your age is astonishing."

"I am thirty!" Flower protests, puffing out her chest. "I am older than you are!"

"Uh..." Diane glances between Flower and the rest of us. "Is she pulling my leg, or...?"

choice(""Yeah, she's just joking. Don't worry about it."", B3-Ch10c-Joking, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Joking)
choice(""Actually, believe it or not, she's telling the truth."", B3-Ch10c-Truth, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Truth)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Joking
TEXT:

 "Yeah, she's just joking. Don't worry about it."

"I am not joking!" Flower shouts. "I am thirty years old!"

The crimson in her eyes vanishes, replaced instantly by glacial blue as Illuna takes control of the expression.

"We had an agreement regarding comments on Flower's age," Illuna says, glaring at me. "If you intend to break it, I politely request you regurgitate the slice of birthday cake you accepted and return it."

"Right. My mistake," I cough into my fist.

"What was that?" Diane's hand drops toward her side. "Her eyes turned vivid blue and her entire posture shifted."

"That is Illuna, the banshee sharing her physical form," I explain. "Their souls fused two decades ago, freezing Flower's biological growth. They reside together within Flower's mind on the ethereal plane, holding daily tea and biscuits."

"A banshee..." Diane studies Flower, her brow furrowing. "That accounts for the dual harmonic in her aura. But housing two distinct wills in a single mind... I had no idea such fusion was possible."

"Hate to cut in," Hadrik says, "but is this the extraction point, or are we marching further?"

 "A short walk," Diane says, turning on her heel. "Follow me."

"Diane, there is so much to tell you," Kate says, falling into step beside her. "You have no idea what has happened. I found Leila. She has been traveling with our company."

"Leila?" Diane pauses, searching her memory. "The mute girl with silver hair who fled with the caretaker? She survived? That is wonderful."

"She communicates through an enchanted interface now," Kate says, her tone warming in a way she rarely lets strangers hear. "It transcribes her thoughts into floating glyphs. We became close. I will introduce you when this is over. You will like her."

"I look forward to it," Diane smiles.

As we hike through the brush, Kate recounts the key points of her exile, her crossing back to Varathia, and our progress through the tournament brackets.

Reaching a barren outcrop, Diane unclips her transceiver and relays our coordinates to the operator below. Once confirmed, she checks that we are set, then gives the signal to engage the array.

Blinding white light wraps around us, accompanied by a resonant hum that vibrates through our teeth. The forest dissolves. We materialize inside a subterranean vault, standing before a teleportation dais similar to the rebel platform in Ollendor, but scaled considerably larger. Behind the control console stands a lessathi technician in formal dark blue robes, observing us with cold scrutiny.

Standing at the edge of the chamber is Meridith herself. I recognize her instantly from the memories revealed during Kate's trial. Her chestnut hair is trimmed shorter, and silver filigree lines the hem of her robes to denote high office, but her green eyes hold the exact steel resolve she carried a decade ago.

She approaches with measured steps, halting directly before Kate.

"You must be Kate," Meridith says.

"I am," Kate answers evenly.

"I see." Meridith lets out a long, quiet breath.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Scepter, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Scepter)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Truth
TEXT:

 "Actually, believe it or not, she's telling the truth."

"She is?" Diane looks from Flower to me. "How can she be thirty? She has the frame of a nine-year-old."

"Her soul was fused with a banshee named Illuna," I explain. "They share the body, living inside Flower's ethereal mind space where they drink tea and eat biscuits every day."

"A banshee..." Diane studies Flower, her brow furrowing. "That accounts for the dual harmonic in her aura. But housing two distinct wills in a single mind... I had no idea such fusion was possible."

"Hate to cut in," Hadrik says, "but is this the extraction point, or are we marching further?"

 "A short walk," Diane says, turning on her heel. "Follow me."

"Diane, there is so much to tell you," Kate says, falling into step beside her. "You have no idea what has happened. I found Leila. She has been traveling with our company."

"Leila?" Diane pauses, searching her memory. "The mute girl with silver hair who fled with the caretaker? She survived? That is wonderful."

"She communicates through an enchanted interface now," Kate says, her tone warming in a way she rarely lets strangers hear. "It transcribes her thoughts into floating glyphs. We became close. I will introduce you when this is over. You will like her."

"I look forward to it," Diane smiles.

As we hike through the brush, Kate recounts the key points of her exile, her crossing back to Varathia, and our progress through the tournament brackets.

Reaching a barren outcrop, Diane unclips her transceiver and relays our coordinates to the operator below. Once confirmed, she checks that we are set, then gives the signal to engage the array.

Blinding white light wraps around us, accompanied by a resonant hum that vibrates through our teeth. The forest dissolves. We materialize inside a subterranean vault, standing before a teleportation dais similar to the rebel platform in Ollendor, but scaled considerably larger. Behind the control console stands a lessathi technician in formal dark blue robes, observing us with cold scrutiny.

Standing at the edge of the chamber is Meridith herself. I recognize her instantly from the memories revealed during Kate's trial. Her chestnut hair is trimmed shorter, and silver filigree lines the hem of her robes to denote high office, but her green eyes hold the exact steel resolve she carried a decade ago.

She approaches with measured steps, halting directly before Kate.

"You must be Kate," Meridith says.

"I am," Kate answers evenly.

"I see." Meridith lets out a long, quiet breath.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Scepter, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Scepter)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Scepter
TEXT:

 Meridith presents an ornate scepter resting across her palms, its shaft etched with geometric bands and crowned with dark gemstones.

"This belongs to you," Meridith says.

"What is it?" Kate's eyes narrow.

"An heirloom of our civilization," Meridith says. "Preserved for over a millennium, from the zenith of the lessathi empire across Varathia. I offer it as restitution for the atrocities inflicted upon you. It cannot undo the horrors of the Beacon, but it stands as an acknowledgment of our debt."

"Sacrilege!" The teleporter engineer slams his hands onto the console. "Unthinkable! The sacred scepter of Heldenbusorag is not a trinket to hand out to surface vagrants! You desecrate our lineage!"

"I desecrate nothing," Meridith says, without raising her voice. "The weight of the relic is precisely what gives the restitution meaning. A hollow gesture would insult us both."

"I cannot take this," Kate steps back. "If it holds that significance to your culture, keep it in your vault."

 "Take it," Meridith says gently, closing Kate's fingers around the grip. "It serves no purpose gathering dust in a reliquary. In the hands of a disciplined elementalist, it sharpens magical compression. Test it."

Kate hesitates, then aims the scepter toward the cavern rock and unleashes a lance of frost.

The icicle punches cleanly through the thick seredium reinforcement, boring a smooth cylindrical tunnel deep into the stone before shattering.

"Remarkable," Kate breathes, examining the weapon. "Structuring an ice projectile to that density normally requires several seconds of focus."

"I am pleased it answers to your touch," Meridith says.

"The Heksol faction will not tolerate this, Lady Meridith," the operator snaps, his knuckles whitening on the controls. "I will report this transgression myself."

 "Save your breath, Grihirt," Meridith replies coolly. "I published my decree the hour I summoned Kate here. If your circle doubted my resolve, seeing the relic in her grip will cure their skepticism. Times evolve. Heksol lies in a grave, yet you cling to his ghost. Reality demands your attention."

"We shall see," Grihirt sneers.

"Follow me," Meridith gestures down the corridor. "The walk to our command center is long."

"Lead on," Daren says, falling in behind her as we leave the fuming engineer behind.

"I understand demanding your presence here for the locator felt heavy-handed," Meridith says as we navigate the torchlit tunnels, "but I intend to ensure this journey rewards your effort. I cannot erase the past, but I can enforce proper compensation."

"Why expend such resources on our behalf?" Illuna asks from Flower's throat. "Tournament stewards claim strict neutrality."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Secret, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Secret)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Secret
TEXT:

 "Diane's companions are under my protection. That extends to you, Illuna of the sacred woods massacre," Meridith says. We exchange glances, realizing she cataloged Flower's passenger long before we stepped off the platform. "Diane's assistance over the decade since the Beacon's closure placed me heavily in her debt. I repay it where I can. If there is anything your party requires, name it."

"I provided no introduction," Illuna remarks. "How did you identify me?"

"I direct lessathi intelligence," Meridith replies. "Tracking significant actors across Varathia is standard protocol. Your pair made an impression over the last twenty years, and rumors tie you to the golden fox. It would be negligence not to know your face."

"Fair enough," Illuna says.

Meridith taps a specific pattern on the seredium wall using a slender metal rod. A concealed seam parts, revealing a passage.

 "You offered assistance," I say as we step through and the partition seals behind us. "Does that cover restricted data? Specifically, the override cipher that unchains the magical attributes on my stat device. I understand only executive lessathi hold that sequence."

"I possess the sequence, but you misunderstand its function," Meridith says as we proceed down the narrow corridor. "The magical pathways in those units do not respond to pure lessathi biology. They activate exclusively for half-lessathi hybrids. That limitation prompted the lockdown in the first place—hybrids were deemed second-class, unfit to wield such capabilities. The modules remained in production because ancient engineers six centuries ago hoped to bypass the restriction, until the still winter wiped them out. Unlocking those stats on your device yields nothing."

"I am half-lessathi," I say. "I activated them during Emergency Mode. They function fine."

"Half-lessathi?" Meridith slows. "Impossible. Interbreeding with humans carries mandatory exile or death under our statutes. And if you entered through the tournament registry, you grew up across the ocean. That implies your parents somehow--"

She freezes in the corridor, turning slowly to look me up and down.

"What is your age?" she asks, her voice dropping.

"Twenty-eight," I answer.

"Twenty-eight years..." Her eyes unfocus, running dates through her mind. "Twenty-eight years since--"

She trails off.

"No. That cannot be."

She raises the metal rod, taps her left shoulder, then reaches out and touches the tip to mine. The metal resonates, flaring with an emerald luminescence. Meridith stares at the glowing rod, rigid.

"The harmonic is identical," she whispers.

"Meaning what?" I ask.

"Meaning we share blood," Meridith says.

choice(""Wait, so does that make me a lessathi prince?"", B3-Ch10c-Prince, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Prince)
choice(""I thought my real parents were supposed to have fled Varathia before I was born."", B3-Ch10c-Fled, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Fled)
choice(""...Mommy?"", B3-Ch10c-Mommy, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Mommy)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Prince
TEXT:

 "Wait, so does that make me a lessathi prince?"

"Our society functions on meritocracy, not hereditary entitlement," Meridith says sharply. "Leadership is earned through achievement. Furthermore, I am your aunt, not your mother. My sister bore you."

"Does this mean you know the fate of my biological parents?" I ask.

"Yes." Meridith dips her chin. "They were executed on Lord Heksol's order for conceiving a hybrid child and fleeing Varathia without authorization."

"How did they get off the island?" I ask. "I thought intercontinental teleportation was restricted to the highest command."

"I assisted their escape," Meridith says. "I served as Heksol's secretary at the time, which granted me access to the activation protocols in his archives. I acquired the coordinates, bribed an operative to stage a perimeter distraction, and saw them through the array."

"And you survived to become leader?" I ask. "How did you avoid an execution cell?"

 "Heksol suspected my involvement immediately, but lacked definitive evidence," Meridith says. "My standing within the ranks was already established, preventing him from acting on assumption alone. He stripped me of my clerical post. Over the following decades, I advanced through competence and results until he had no choice but to appoint me his second."

She pauses, looking down at the wand.

"I failed to search for you," Meridith says quietly. "When my sister departed, we severed all communication to ensure her location could never be extracted from me under interrogation. I uncovered the execution reports years later, filed away in Heksol's legacy archives. I possessed no name, no continent, no certainty you survived infancy. My only recourse was carrying this resonance rod on diplomatic crossings, hoping for an emerald signal. To have you materialize here, unbidden..."

"What happens now?" I ask. "Do we embrace?"

 "No," Meridith says, the executive steel returning to her posture. "Though your arrival is remarkably opportune. There is someone essential you must meet. I intended to bring Kate before him, but your identity makes your presence equally vital."

"Who?" I ask. "Another lessathi officer?"

"You will see shortly," Meridith says, turning down the passage. "We have no time to squander."

As we follow her through the abandoned corridors, a chill ripples across my shoulders. Eiden's parting words ring clear in my memory:

"Aren't you pleased, Barry? Now you finally have the opportunity to deliver my message."

Eiden's message.

'Remember the still winter.' Phrased like a direct threat. How does Meridith take those words? Imprisonment? Retaliation? Or is it something else? Stillwaters fought lessathi six centuries ago, but Melindra claimed Eiden took the lessathi side. Perhaps it serves as a warning rather than a challenge.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Phrasing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Phrasing)


