choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Phrasing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Phrasing)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Phrasing
TEXT:

 Ignoring Eiden's instructions is not an option. The last time I neglected his courier duty, he nearly tore us apart. I have no desire to test his wrath twice.

"Meridith," I call out as we march through the gloom. "This is abrupt, but I carry a message for you. From Eiden."

"From Eiden?" Meridith stops. She does not turn around. "What words could he have for me?"

"Remember the still winter."

Silence falls heavy in the corridor. She stands motionless.

"That was all," I add into the quiet. "He said you would understand the meaning."

"You are certain of the phrasing?" she asks, resuming her stride with slow, calculated steps. "Word for word?"

"Word for word," I say, keeping pace.

"Did he direct this to me specifically?"

 "Not originally," I admit. "I was instructed to deliver it to the first lessathi I encountered. I was half-dead at the time and failed to pass it along. Eiden took offense when he realized I had crossed paths with your people and said nothing, so I made sure to discharge the debt now."

"How did he know you had met lessathi?" Meridith asks. "Did you report the encounter?"

"He read it off us," I say. "His magical perception covers the entire island. He detected the lessathi resonance lingering on our auras."

"What did you say?" Meridith whips around, staring directly into my eyes.

"About what?"

"You claim Eiden identified lessathi operatives simply by scanning their aura signatures?"

 "Yes," I say. "His range is absurd."

"You do not grasp the implication," Meridith says, her composure cracking. "Every operative on the surface wears an enchanted cuff under their cuff to mask their presence. If Eiden cuts through that shielding at distance, our field agents are completely exposed."

"Gwahaha!" Arraka cackles from the amulet. "You believed those cheap copper bands would blind Eiden? You would need to amplify their density a hundredfold to baffle a stillwater of his caliber!"

"A hundredfold..." Meridith absorbs the mockery without flinching, already calculating. "We can cannibalize redundant units and consolidate the warding cores. It will leave fewer scouts equipped, but those deployed will remain hidden. I will assign the workshop to reconfigure the prototypes immediately."

"Good luck with that," Arraka snorts.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Deserved, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Deserved)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Deserved
TEXT:

 "I don't understand why Eiden's tracking concerns you so deeply," I say as we push farther down the shaft. "Was he not an ally to the lessathi during the still winter?"

"An ally?" Meridith's tone hardens instantly. "Eiden was the spark that ignited the slaughter. Am I expected to bow in gratitude because he switched allegiances in the eleventh hour, after his brethren had butchered the bulk of our civilization? The still winter was no war. It was extermination, and every stillwater who drew breath carries that butcher's stain."

"Hold on," Melindra cuts in, her voice flaring. "Neither I, nor my brother, nor Eiden took part in that slaughter. And your people invited that catastrophe. If the lessathi had not caged Eiden and the other casters like cattle, events would never have erupted into--"

"Our fault?" Meridith wheels on her, fury cold and sharp in her eyes. "The women? The infants in their cradles? You claim they earned their slaughter?"

 "I--" Melindra falters, the words sticking in her throat. "I do not endorse what the stillwaters did. I am stating they were not the instigators."

"Hold up," I interject. "Were those casters imprisoned because lessathi researchers were experimenting on them to manufacture stillwaters? Is that what triggered the collapse?"

"None hold the truth of their creation save the stillwaters themselves," Meridith says, casting a disdainful look at Melindra. "Eiden ensured the methodology was obliterated so no human could ever rise to challenge his supremacy."

"So the recipe is lost to you as well," I murmur. "Interesting."

 Meridith taps the end of the shaft with her rod. Another seam divides, ushering us into a corridor blazing with sapphire radiance. Blue crystals line the stone from floor to ceiling. As in Ollendor's deep caverns, the minerals pulse with a slow, rhythmic cadence, though the thrum feels less volatile than four days ago.

"That is a massive concentration of crystals," Hadrik remarks as the stone seals behind us.

"Indeed," Meridith says, turning down the right fork where the clusters thicken. "If the pulsation alarms you, they have maintained this harmonic for four days without incident. We continue to investigate the resonance trigger, without breakthrough."

choice("Tell Meridith that everything began after the king of Ollendor blew up the caves.", B3-Ch10c-Brightly, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Brightly, v_b3_ch10_crystals = 1)
choice("Don't tell Meridith anything about the crystals.", B3-Ch10c-Brightly, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Brightly, v_b3_ch10_crystals = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Brightly
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_crystals == 1) {
 "We know the trigger," I say. "The resonance began when the king of Ollendor detonated the subterranean caverns beneath the city to crush the rebels."

"You claim these formations reacted to an explosion across the continent?" Meridith asks, studying me.

"The pattern matches," I say. "The Ollendor deposits fell into the exact same rhythm—a slow dimming and flaring, like a collective pulse."

"If verified, that correlation will advance our research considerably," Meridith says. "I appreciate the disclosure."

"Glad to help," I say.

"We have not encountered a single lessathi since the teleportation room," Kate observes as we navigate the sapphire-lit tunnel.

"These thoroughfares remain restricted to executive ranks," Meridith says. "Transit requires my direct authorization."

"A council chamber, then?" Daren asks.

"No," Meridith says quietly. "A consecrated sanctum."

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_crystals == 0) {
 I keep the Ollendor detonation to myself. Handing over tactical intelligence before understanding her motives is bad practice.

"We have not encountered a single lessathi since the teleportation room," Kate observes as we navigate the sapphire-lit tunnel.

"These thoroughfares remain restricted to executive ranks," Meridith says. "Transit requires my direct authorization."

"A council chamber, then?" Daren asks.

"No," Meridith says quietly. "A consecrated sanctum."

}
 She halts before an unadorned seredium partition, free of crystal growth, and turns to face us.

"We have arrived," Meridith says. "The entity awaiting your presence resides beyond this threshold."

"You are finally going to name our host?" Hadrik asks.

"Yes," Meridith says, raising her wand to the metal surface. "A presence you know by legend, though few living souls have stood before it."

The wall responds to the wand's contact, the heavy slab retracting smoothly into the ceiling.

"The ultimate consciousness," Meridith intones as the chamber opens. "The guide of our people since antiquity."

Within the vast chamber sits a colossal sphere of solid blue radiance, churning endlessly around a core of impenetrable darkness.

"Behold our true Creator," Meridith says. "The Magium."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Panicked, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Panicked)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Panicked
TEXT:

 "Run!" Arraka screeches, her voice cracking inside the amulet. "Get back, you morons! Run before it--"

Blue luminescence erupts from the sphere, surging across the floor and swallowing us whole.

The cavern vanishes. Weight, direction, and breath cease to exist. We drift suspended in an unmapped void, shapes without contours, my companions turning in blind confusion. Before my mind can anchor itself, the fabric of space shreds, and I plummet into the absolute nothingness of the void—the same erasure where the Magium hurled me before.

Sheer terror grips my consciousness. I blink in and out of reality, ripped between nonexistence and the Magium's conceptual realm. With every pulse, my companions reappear beside me, drenched in cold sweat, eyes wide with horror as their minds buckle against the erasure.

"Barry..." Kate's voice trembles, thin and fragile. "Is this the void you spoke of? I heard your description, but... this is worse than any nightmare..."

"Arraka, move!" Daren shouts, fighting to keep his voice from breaking. "Cast the primordial ward!"

 "I cannot!" Arraka screams from the amulet. "The spell finds no purchase! There is no entity to target! It is everywhere and nowhere!"

The void tears at my consciousness again, stronger this time, threatening to pull my existence apart permanently.

"Magium, stop!" Flower cries out, terror breaking through her voice. "Petal and I helped you before! We can help again! Tell us what you need! Please!"

The oscillation ceases. We float motionless in the impossible geometry of the Magium's realm. Space refuses definition; perspective bends in directions that possess no names. Time bleeds forward and backward simultaneously—I perceive our entry as a single moment, yet remember it as an ancient, repeating cycle unfolding in reverse.

The entity answers Flower, its tone shifting with each syllable, weaving through different voices.

"In the past, you offered aid," speaks the voice of an aged man.

"In the present, you have failed," continues a woman's measured cadence.

"The prophecy has been spoken," says an adolescent youth.

"You understand what must be done," chimes a young girl.

"We don't!" Flower insists frantically. "We don't understand! We haven't even identified the two ancient adversaries clashing! We assume Eiden is one, because he made it obvious, but the other--"

"Then you hold your answer," a woman's soft murmur cuts in.

"To preserve the multitude, a single life must be forfeit," speaks the halting voice of a small child.

"A sacrifice?" Flower whispers. "You... you demand we execute Eiden?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Guidance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Guidance)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Guidance
TEXT:

 "The prophecy must not come to pass," a man's voice rings out sharp and clear.

"If you require direction, the one named Meridith will instruct you," an elderly woman speaks.

"Do not fail again!" an old man roars.

Reality snaps shut. In the fraction of a heartbeat, the Magium expels us from its domain, dropping us back onto the stone floor before the rotating sphere of blue light. Everyone stumbles, shaking off disorientation.

"Astonishing," Meridith breathes, watching us with rapt fascination. "I have communed with our Creator on numerous occasions, yet he never granted me audience within his personal realm. Your eyes flared with brilliant luminescence, exactly as recorded in ancient chronicles."

"Right..." Daren steadies his footing. "You had no foreknowledge of this?"

 "None," Meridith says. "You require privacy to process an encounter of this magnitude. I will grant you several minutes while I arrange the council chamber down the adjoining hall. When you conclude your communion, close the barrier by tapping the seredium five times. I await you at the corridor's end. Take whatever time you need."

"Understood," Daren mutters. "We will take our time."

"Diane, accompany me to prepare the chamber," Meridith instructs.

"Right away," Diane says, throwing a quick wave over her shoulder as she follows Meridith into the passage. "See you shortly."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Scattered, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Scattered)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Scattered
TEXT:

 As Meridith and Diane depart down the hall, the others lean against the walls, drawing ragged breaths to steady their racing pulses. Meanwhile, my attention pulls inexorably back toward the rotating sphere. An invisible gravity tugs at my thoughts.

The ultimate objective of the tournament sits three paces from me. Walk away now, and the chance may never return.

"A Magium node buried this deep," Arraka's voice mutters from the amulet. "Sloppy. I allowed the seredium shielding to blind my magical sense."

"What is a Magium node?" Hadrik asks. "The floating light construct?"

"A focal point," Arraka explains. "Several exist across Varathia, linked through the underlying planar network. Major factions hold them in balance. It stands to reason the lessathi secured one of their own, however carefully they concealed it."

 "Which factions?" Daren asks. "Tyrath? Eiden? The golden fox?"

"Those three, plus the reigning monarchs and Fyron," Arraka says. "A single node provides complete access to the Magium, so distribution remained stable without territorial wars. Furthermore, their locations are--"

"Barry, step away from that!" Daren shouts.

I ignore him, moving closer until the radiance washes over my skin. Up close, the construct towers over me. The core is an absolute, lightless void, while solid sapphire bands orbit in perpetual motion, casting brilliant illumination without dispelling the central dark.

 The structure commands total focus, pulling at my senses until the surrounding cavern fades.

"Barry!" Daren's voice cracks with urgency. "Do not do something catastrophic! Back up slowly!"

"He is standing on the threshold!" Arraka's laughter rings out. "Touch it, weakling! I dare you!"

choice("Touch the Magium node.", B3-Ch10c-Colors, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Colors, v_b3_ch10_node = 1)
choice("Back away from the Magium node.", B3-Ch10c-Colors, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Colors, v_b3_ch10_node = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Colors
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 1) {
 I reach out and press my palm directly against the rotating sphere of light and void.

A tidal wave of billions of voices, concepts, and visions crashes into my mind simultaneously. For a single suspended heartbeat, the comprehensive blueprint of reality unfolds before me. Yet the instant I reach for comprehension, the knowledge recedes into infinite distance, remaining unreachable however far my mind stretches.

I yank my hand back before the pressure fractures my sanity. The flood of universal mechanics evaporates as quickly as it arrived.

Yet a profound shift remains. The raw data has fled, but its foundation lingers—a permanent imprint burned into the bedrock of my memory, abandoned when the connection severed.

If I could decipher even a fraction of that residue...

"Are you completely out of your mind?" Daren grabs my tunic, hauling me backward out of the chamber. "Barry, you will put me in an early grave!"

 He strikes the seredium wall five times in rapid succession. The heavy door grinds shut, sealing the node behind solid metal.

"Well?" Daren demands, catching his breath as I stand motionless. "What happened when you touched it?"

"A fraction of a second," I say, reassembling my thoughts. "I touched the node and perceived the entire architecture of existence. But holding on would have dissolved my mind. Real madness, not a figure of speech. The information was laid out, but processing it would demand an eternity. Releasing it was the only way to avoid cerebral collapse."

"Remarkable restraint on your part," Daren remarks, eyebrows raised.

"I have survival instincts," I retort.

"Good to know," Daren says. "Did you retain anything useful?"

"Hard to tell," I say. "Fragments remain, but decoding them will take time."

"In other words, nothing immediate," Daren concludes.

 "A question," Kate says, glancing uneasily at the sealed partition. "Can the entity monitor our words through the red sphere with the seredium door closed?"

"Doubtful," Arraka says. "Though putting distance between us and the threshold is wise."

"Red sphere?" Hadrik interrupts, frowning as we move down the corridor. "What red sphere? The construct was yellow and black. Where did you find red?"

"The light was green," Daren says. "Perception must vary by observer. Correct, Arraka?"

"Accurate," Arraka confirms. "Flower and Illuna perceive violet. I have possessed enough vessels to observe every hue on the spectrum."

"Yet the crystal formations are blue to everyone," Kate points out. "If they are crystallized Magium essence, why does their color not shift?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 0) {
 I step backward, letting my hand fall to my side. Poking an omnipotent planar construct before hearing Meridith's proposal is an unnecessary gamble.

"Thank the gods," Daren exhales. "I thought you were going to grab it."

"The temptation was there," I say, "but testing an unknown artifact without preparation is reckless."

"Coward," Arraka snorts from the amulet.

Daren strikes the seredium partition five times, and the heavy slab grinds shut, cutting off the radiance.

"Can the entity monitor our words through the red sphere with the seredium door closed?" Kate asks.

"Doubtful," Arraka says. "Though putting distance between us and the threshold is wise."

 "Red sphere?" Hadrik interrupts, frowning as we move down the corridor. "What red sphere? The construct was yellow and black. Where did you find red?"

"The light was green," Daren says. "Perception must vary by observer. Correct, Arraka?"

"Accurate," Arraka confirms. "Flower and Illuna perceive violet. I have possessed enough vessels to observe every hue on the spectrum."

"Yet the crystal formations are blue to everyone," Kate points out. "If they are crystallized Magium essence, why does their color not shift?"

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Common, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Common)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Common
TEXT:

 "The crystal mechanics are obscure," Arraka says, "but blue remains the most frequent baseline for node perception. Among the animals I inhabited over the centuries, blue manifested far more than any other shade."

"Fitting, since blue is what I saw," I remark.

"A common color for an ordinary fellow," Hadrik chuckles. "The cosmos has a sense of humor."

"I have made peace with it," I shrug.

"Can we address what occurred in that realm?" Daren asks, glancing back at the group. "I have no desire to reopen wounds, but ignoring the demand is dangerous."

"It is straightforward," Kate says. "Barry prepared us for the void, which blunted the shock. But the substance of the encounter is clear. The Magium verified Eiden as one of the two figures in the prophecy and directed us to eliminate him."

"So we become assassins on command?" Hadrik asks, folding his arms. "What leverage does that sphere hold over us if we simply avoid its chambers?"

 "Selkram's trials link you directly to the Magium via the time weaver," Arraka explains. "During prophetic transmission, you stand upon its planar threshold. Avoiding the nodes does not eliminate exposure."

"I am no one's lackey," Hadrik rumbles. "If the Magium wishes to erase me, let it try. If circumstances force a confrontation with Eiden, fine. But I will not hunt a man because a floating light ordered it."

"Spoken like a champion," Arraka jeers. "I will ensure blankets reach your cell in the void. It grows drafty during eternity."

"Skip the blankets and send dwarven stout," Hadrik says. "Better insulation."

"Noted," Arraka laughs.

#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 1) {
 "I understand something critical," I say, stepping between them after running the impressions through my thoughts.

"Do enlighten us," Arraka says dryly.

"When I touched the node, the connection went deeper than the ritual link," I say. "It was not simply a conduit to magical power. It was union with the foundation. The source."

"The source of magic?" Daren asks.

"Not just magic," I say. "The source of the physical realm, the magical plane, the ethereal plane. Every parallel divergence. Time, geometry, consciousness, and volition. The source of everything."

"You claim that is the true definition of the Magium?" Kate asks. "Not the root of magic, but the origin of all existence?"

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Informed, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Informed)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Informed
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch10_forbidden,1) if (v_b3_ch10_node == 1)
achievement("Forbidden knowledge",v_ac_b3_ch10_forbidden)
#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 1) {
 "Gwahahaha!" Arraka's laughter explodes through the tunnel. "Precisely. 'Source of everything.' Those were the exact three words I withheld, because speaking them aloud reveals its nature. Since neither of you has vanished into nonexistence, we may discuss the concept openly—provided we stay clear of active nodes and Selkram's rituals. Though taking this long to deduce the obvious is embarrassing."

"The behavior of these fragments makes little sense," Daren says. "If their mandate is guarding that secret, why did the entity release us? Arraka nearly disclosed the truth in its presence before."

 "Fragments operate under isolated directives," Arraka says. "Each pursues a singular objective, blind to the rest. This entity is tasked with suppressing the prophecy. Lacking the ability to manifest physically without tearing reality apart—like the fragment driving the Overseer—it manipulates mortal agents. The entity that targeted me during Selkram's ritual was a localized defense mechanism guarding the threshold."

"Then this fragment held no awareness of your prior near-disclosure?" Hadrik asks.

"They do not communicate," Arraka says. "It attacked purely to enforce obedience regarding the prophecy. That marks the third fragment to threaten our erasure. Our mortal lives mean nothing to them."

"A single sacrifice to preserve the multitude," Daren quotes quietly. "Collateral damage."

 "Indeed," Arraka says. "And the other fragment would have unmade the world to secure my past self. These entities crush whatever obstructs their objective. Avoiding their gaze remains our best strategy."

"The council room lies ahead," Kate says, pointing toward an arched doorway. "Shall we enter?"

"We must decide whether to disclose the Magium's command to Meridith," Daren says under his breath.

"You mean the order to murder Eiden?" Melindra whispers sharply. "You cannot seriously entertain that. He will slaughter you."

"We are deciding our stance for the meeting," Daren clarifies. "Hearing her position first may guide us, or we allow her to open the subject. Thoughts?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 0) {
 "I feel as though a vital truth slipped through my fingers," I mutter. "As though the definition of the Magium was within reach, and I stepped back."

"Doubtless," Arraka mocks. "Had you shown spine and grasped the node, the secret would be yours. Now you carry that ignorance to the grave."

"Ignore her," Daren tells me. "You chose prudence. An uncontrolled surge could have destroyed you."

"Right," I say. "Prudence."

We reach the reinforced portal at the corridor's end. Daren pauses with his hand near the handle.

"We must decide whether to disclose the Magium's command to Meridith," Daren says under his breath.

"You mean the order to murder Eiden?" Melindra whispers sharply. "You cannot seriously entertain that. He will slaughter you."

"We are deciding our stance for the meeting," Daren clarifies. "Hearing her position first may guide us, or we allow her to open the subject. Thoughts?"

}
choice(""I think it would be better to mention it to Meridith."", B3-Ch10c-Decorations, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Decorations, v_b3_ch10_mention = 1)
choice(""We should probably avoid mentioning any of this to Meridith, for now."", B3-Ch10c-Decorations, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Decorations, v_b3_ch10_mention = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Decorations
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_mention == 1) {
 "Better to bring it up," I say. "Meridith likely knows the fragment's directive. Concealing it might raise suspicion."

"Agreed," Daren says. "The Magium instructed us to seek her guidance. Does anyone object?"

"Makes no difference to me," Hadrik shrugs. "I am sitting out the manhunt regardless."

"Fine by me," Kate says, and Flower nods agreement.

"Then we proceed," Daren says, pushing the heavy door inward.

We file into a chamber reminiscent of the underground king's audience hall in Ollendor, though the stonework here bears intricate lessathi reliefs featuring the heraldic hawk clutching the sun.

A long conference table dominates the room, flanked by chairs. Meridith and Diane sit on the far side, watching our entrance. Meridith gestures toward the vacant seats opposite them, and we take our places across the polished surface.

Once we settle, Meridith leans forward.

 "You wonder why I brought you to this installation," Meridith says. "Answers will follow, but I must ask a preliminary question. You are under no compulsion to respond, and forgive the directness, but what transpired during your communion with our Creator?"

We exchange brief glances. Daren leans forward to speak for the table.

"The Magium instructed us to eliminate Eiden, to prevent the catastrophe foretold in the prophecy," Daren says.

"Then my hypothesis was accurate," Meridith says, a sharp satisfaction entering her tone. "He invited you to our cause."

"Your cause?" Daren asks. "You received the same mandate?"

"I did," Meridith says. "And I have directed our resources toward that objective for years."

"Before we proceed," I cut in, "I have a question of my own."

"Ask," Meridith invites. "Nothing is withheld."

"Nothing?" I lean back, meeting her eyes. "In that case, confirm whether you orchestrated the continent-wide buyout of stat devices prior to the tournament."

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_mention == 0) {
 "Keep the Magium's command to ourselves for now," I say. "Volunteering that detail might suggest we have already signed on to the assassination. I want to hear her pitch first."

"Sensible," Daren agrees. "Any dissent, or do we follow Barry's lead?"

"Makes no difference to me," Hadrik shrugs. "I am sitting out the manhunt regardless."

"Fine by me," Kate says, and Flower nods agreement.

"Then we proceed," Daren says, pushing the heavy door inward.

We file into a chamber reminiscent of the underground king's audience hall in Ollendor, though the stonework here bears intricate lessathi reliefs featuring the heraldic hawk clutching the sun.

A long conference table dominates the room, flanked by chairs. Meridith and Diane sit on the far side, watching our entrance. Meridith gestures toward the vacant seats opposite them, and we take our places across the polished surface.

Once we settle, Meridith leans forward.

 "You wonder why I brought you to this installation," Meridith says. "Answers will follow, but I must ask a preliminary question. You are under no compulsion to respond, and forgive the directness, but what transpired during your communion with our Creator?"

We exchange brief glances. Daren answers smoothly.

"We prefer to keep that confidential for the moment," he says.

"Understood," Meridith says without missing a beat. "Take your time. If you choose to discuss it later, my door remains open."

"Thank you," Daren says.

"Before we proceed," I cut in, "I have a question of my own."

"Ask," Meridith invites. "Nothing is withheld."

"Nothing?" I lean back, meeting her eyes. "In that case, confirm whether you orchestrated the continent-wide buyout of stat devices prior to the tournament."

}
 Silence hangs over the table. Meridith holds my gaze, her expression unreadable.

"What led you to that deduction?" she asks, voice completely level.

"First," I say, "whoever organized the buyout needed immense capital, recognized the units were far more than fortune teller novelties, and required them before the tournament commenced. The list of candidates matching that profile is short."

She does not blink or shift.

"Continue," she says. "What else supports your hypothesis?"

"Energy draw," I say. "My stat booster harvests significantly less charge inside Thilias and Ollendor than out in the wilderness. Furthermore, another participant who remained static in Thilias accumulated far fewer points than I did. If thousands of harvesting devices were saturated beneath those cities, the distribution drop makes complete sense."

"What are you implying?" Meridith asks.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Sham, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Sham)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Sham
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch7_overheard == 0) {
 "What I am stating plainly," I say, "is that this tournament is an elaborate fiction. You never intended to award the Magium to a victor. You dangled it because nothing else on this earth could lure hundreds of foreign mages into one territory. The contest is not a defense trial for Varathia. It is a harvesting net, using converted stat boosters to siphon raw magical energy from every competitor who set foot on this island."

"The delay," Hadrik grunts, his eyes narrowing. "That week we sat in Thilias doing nothing. The pinecone hunt across the wilderness. Stalling for time so the machines could drain us dry."

"Exactly," I say. "The only detail that eludes me is why you allowed contestants to slaughter one another in the field. Enforce a ceasefire before the opening round, and you could have siphoned them indefinitely."

 Meridith listens without a flicker of surprise, resting her chin on linked fingers. When she speaks, her voice carries the calm authority of someone reciting notes from a meeting she ran yesterday.

"That was our initial architecture," she says smoothly. "We installed the primary extractor arrays beneath the continental capitals, calculating that the majority of foreign casters would congregate inside the city walls while awaiting instructions. We disabled the central telepathic relay ourselves to stretch the waiting window. The flaw was the monarchs. The kings of Varathia rejected a non-lethal mandate, declaring bloodshed an inviolable tournament tradition—and delivered that refusal hours before the opening bell. You cannot fathom the logistical disruption that caused."

"You admit this?" Daren stares at her, bewildered. "Without a shred of hesitation?"

"Why maintain a pretense?" Meridith counters, her eyes locking onto mine. "Your companion dismantled the puzzle. Feigning ignorance wastes time, and I have far more productive uses for this audience."

}
#if(v_b2_ch7_overheard == 1) {
 "What I am stating plainly," I say, "is that this tournament is an elaborate fiction. You never intended to award the Magium to a victor. You dangled it because nothing else on this earth could lure hundreds of foreign mages into one territory. The contest is not a defense trial for Varathia. It is a harvesting net, using converted stat boosters to siphon raw magical energy from every competitor who set foot on this island."

"The delay," Hadrik grunts, his eyes narrowing. "That week we sat in Thilias doing nothing. The pinecone hunt across the wilderness. Stalling for time so the machines could drain us dry."

"Exactly," I say. "Your operatives in Thilias were the ones who sabotaged the telepathic relay to buy time. I heard one complaining that the kings refused a non-lethal rule. You needed participants breathing so your machines could harvest maximum charge."

Meridith's gaze sharpens. "The Thilias outpost? Sloppy. I expected better discretion from field operatives."

 "They spoke archaic lessathi in an empty thoroughfare," I say. "They had no reason to suspect a tournament participant held enhanced hearing and ancient dialects on his booster."

"Did you just confirm his deductions?" Daren asks, incredulous. "You orchestrated the entire fraud?"

"I did," Meridith says, her posture unyielding. "Denial serves no purpose when your deduction is sound. Concealing what you have already uncovered is an inefficient use of our time."

}
 "However," Meridith continues, tapping one manicured finger on the table, "your analysis contains two critical errors. First, we did not bury thousands of commercial stat boosters in the earth. We acquired those units to strip their components, constructing industrial-scale extraction arrays far more potent than hand-held relics. Second, I never stated you would be denied access to the Magium."

"You expect us to believe that?" I challenge. "You just confirmed the tournament is a fabricated harvest, yet you claim the prize remains real?"

"You misunderstand the offer," Meridith says, leaning forward. "The eventual tournament winner receives nothing. That spectacle ends in ash. But I am prepared to grant you access to our private node. You, and every member of your party. In exchange for a specific service."

"What service?" Kate asks, her fingers tightening on the scepter.

"Consider the origin," Meridith says, her voice dropping into cold, deliberate clarity. "This entire tournament—the alliances, the siphons, the deception—exists for one solitary target. Ever since the destruction of Olmnar twenty years ago, the lessathi high command and the ruling kings have operated under a unified pact. After two decades of preparation, the apparatus is complete. By the tournament's conclusion, justice will be executed. Eiden will die."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Ending
TEXT:

 "You want us to kill Eiden for you?" Hadrik asks.

"No," Meridith replies. "Our primary strike will execute the operation. That is the sole destination for the energy we harvest."

"Then what role do we serve?" Daren asks.

"Contingency," Meridith says. "If the past weeks proved anything, it is that meticulous planning can collapse under unforeseen fortune. I require insurance. This is no suicide errand. Should Eiden survive our opening barrage through some miracle, he will be shattered—his reserves drained, his wards fractured. Weakened to a degree your company can dismantle him with minimal risk. If you agree to act as that contingency, I will grant you unrestricted access to the Magium node we visited."

"Will you disclose your operational plan?" Daren asks.

"I will not," Meridith states plainly. "If Eiden captures your company and extracts that intelligence, twenty years of preparation evaporates. I cannot accept that exposure."

 "You promise Magium access if we intervene," Kate says, "but what if your plan executes flawlessly? If your strike destroys Eiden without our involvement, what then?"

"The reward stands," Meridith says. "Serving as our contingency requires you to maintain your presence on Varathia until the tournament concludes. That means progressing through the brackets and fulfilling objectives so the monarchs have no pretext to deport you. Fulfill that obligation, and your prize is guaranteed regardless of whether your blades are drawn."

"And the kings know nothing of this pact?" Kate asks.

"Nothing," Meridith confirms. "The royal courts are unaware we possess a functional node. They believe access is strictly monopolized by the crown. This covenant is struck with me, as high leader of the lessathi, independent of tournament stewardship. Maintain your standing, stand ready if our primary strike falters, and all of you claim the prize you crossed the ocean to find, without needing to win the tournament."

 She rises from her seat, smoothing her robes with deliberate composure, her gaze sweeping across each of us in turn.

"The terms are laid before you," Meridith says. "The choice remains yours."

Silence settles over the chamber as she pauses, letting the weight of the pact fill the room.

"What is your answer?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Credits, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Credits)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Credits
TEXT:

 The eleventh chapter of the third book is now available, however it is not the final chapter of the book. This book will have 12 chapters in total.

I am very sorry for the huge delay. I've had a very bad stress-induced writer's block that started with the invasion of Ukraine (due to living in a neighboring country), and I haven't been able to regain the same writing speed since then.

I realize this isn't really an excuse, and I'm trying hard to regain a decent writing speed, but for the moment I had to split the remaining content until the end of book 3 into two separate chapters, due to the long distance between the checkpoints, so I decided to publish 'chapter 11' separately.

The final chapter was always meant to be split into two parts with a time skip between them, like chapter 10, so you can at least rest assured that the ending of chapter 11 won't leave you on a big cliffhanger.

Also, due to the many delays I've had in the past, I've decided to make the estimate much more vague this time, because I still don't know how fast I'll be able to write.

